      <workList xml:id="workDesc_7d4118aa">
         <work xml:id="work_idee87741a">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_d73aaab9">C4:011</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id634f2f47" xml:lang="de">Peter Paars, ein comisches Heldengedicht</title>
            <title type="original" xml:id="title_587bb436" xml:lang="da">Peder Paars, Poema heroico-comicum</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_24bd6859">
               <persName role="author" xml:id="persName_f6be8b7a">Ludvig Holberg</persName>
               <persName role="translator" xml:id="persName_idf5c2a0dc">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_iddd6e0512">
               <date isodate="1750" xml:id="date_32de0a47">1750</date>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_id849debea">
               <p xml:id="idm129">In 1740, already shortly after his arrival in Copenhagen, Scheibe became acquainted with the professor at the university and playwright <persName xml:id="idm130" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Ludvig Holberg</persName> who was also an able musical amateur. Holberg’s comic hero epic, <rend xml:id="idm131" fontstyle="italic">Peder Paars</rend>, was issued in four parts 1719–20 as a grand parody on Virgil’s <rend xml:id="idm132" fontstyle="italic">Aeneid</rend>, modelling the hero on <persName xml:id="idm133" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Cervantes</persName>’<rend xml:id="idm134" fontstyle="italic"> Don Quixote</rend>. When Scheibe settled in Sønderborg he began in earnest to translate works into German of which one of the first must have been <rend xml:id="idm135" fontstyle="italic">Peder Paars</rend>. He appears to have started in 1749, and finished the translation already five months later; the publishers had given him six months but due to other projects he could spent no more time on it, though he would have liked to have done so with such a difficult work: 'The publisher only gave me a very short time for this poetic work. It is therefore a hasty work rather than a complete one with much diligence and long reflection. Of the six months that I had been granted at the maximum for that purpose, I have not been able to work even five months on it due to other jobs. And such a short time for translating such an extensive poem is by no means sufficient' (sig. d6r–v). In late September 1749, he had thus already handed over the first part to the publishers and before Christmas the two last ones. In a letter to his friend, <persName xml:id="idm136" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName>, Scheibe mentions that since June 1749 and until January 1750 he had been working on the translation of <rend xml:id="idm137" fontstyle="italic">Peder Paars</rend> and that the work would be published for the Easter fair. Finally, Scheibe dated his foreword February 1750 – a foreword which includes an extensive summary of the satirical epic as well as information regarding the translation. During summer 1750 Scheibe visited Hamburg and spoke with <persName xml:id="idm138" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Friedrich von Hagedorn</persName> who praised the translation. A review of the work appeared in <rend xml:id="idm139" fontstyle="italic">Jenaische gelehrte Zeitungen</rend> in September which made Scheibe happy. However, due to the pressure of time under which the work had been carried out, Scheibe was well aware that there were a number of errors in the edition and that the translation might seem crude and hard (‘rauch und hart’). Therefore, in 1764 a completely new and revised translation appeared. Scheibe believed, however, that the original <rend xml:id="idm140" fontstyle="italic">Peder Paars</rend> had a defect in part 3, between songs 2 and 3, and he therefore added a new section (p. xviii). A new foreword was included, not only dealing with the problems of translating the work but also presenting the first ever detailed biography of Holberg who had died ten years earlier. The revised translation of 1764 was dedicated to Lord Chamberlain, <persName xml:id="idm141" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Adam Gottlob Moltke</persName>, thanking him for favours shown Scheibe and his son: 'I hereby take the opportunity to express my humble gratitude to Your Noble Excellency for so many well-found favours shown to me and my son [<persName xml:id="idm142" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Christian Friedrich</persName>]; and still further to recommend myself with high grace and kindness of servility to Your Noble Excellency' (sig. a3v; see also SchW B2:431). The translation was reviewed mentioning that German readers, 'who do not understand the greater part of the comic jokes and what they mean, cannot find them pleasing; especially since, as we must admit, Mr. S. has no particular gift for writing poetry. The appendix on Holberg’s life may please those who have not read his own Latin autobiography. Anyway, we fear that Mr Scheibe has so little talent for biography as for the art of poetry. It is true that he describes everything that has happened, very accurate and correct, but he does not distinguish important events from the unimportant [‘schlecht’], [and] his manner of writing is dull and slow. In short, he has all the faults of the common German story writing.' Scheibe's foreword of this edition is interesting, revealing a few autobiographical facts.</p>
            </history>
